==German==

===Etymology===
During the middle ages people couldnt afford a full meal at the breweries in Cologne anymore due to the bad economy, so the brewers decided to offer a cheaper alternative. ''Kann isch ne halve han?'' means 'Can I get a half (of a sandwich)'; foreigners interpreted this as ''Halve Hahn'' ('half a rooster'). Nowadays, it's often written like this on the menus of rustical restaurants in Cologne.

===Noun===
'''Halve Hahn''' {{m}}

# {{context|Cologne dialect|lang=de}} a [[cheese]] [[sandwich]], cut in half.

